## INTRODUCTION
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have great potential in a many applications, such as gas separation and storage1-4, catalysis5,6, sensing7 and drug delivery8-10. Their advantageous properties include structural diversity, large surface area, and tunable pore sizes and functionalities11-15. MOFs have been used as carriers of metal nanoparticles for catalysis,16,17 and of organic molecules such as drugs9 and proteins18 for medical applications. The encapsulation of target molecules by MOFs is currently achieved using a process that includes several steps: 1) synthesis of MOFs, 2) removal of solvents from the pores, and 3) incorporation of target molecules, which are often dissolved in toxic organic solvents. This process is both costly and produces large amounts of waste. Another drawback is the small pore window of MOFs, which limits their potential to encapsulate large molecules. In addition, the current loading capacity of large molecules in MOFs is lower than that of other inorganic carriers, such as mesoporous silica. A strategy to overcome these drawbacks is to combine MOF synthesis and molecule encapsulation into a one-pot 18-22   
process
Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are a subclass of MOFs, with high thermal and hydrothermal stabilities23-26. ZIFs have been used in gas separation27 and as carriers for metal nanoparticles28 and drugs29. ZIF-8 is a non-toxic and biocompatible ZIF built from zinc ions and 2- methylimidazolate, and that is a potential carrier for anticancer drugs30,31. ZIF-8 is stable under physiological conditions and decomposes under acidic conditions, which can be used to construct pH-sensitive drug delivery systems29. However, the diameter of the pore opening of ZIF-8 is $3.4\textup{\AA}$ and the diameter of the pore cavity is $11.6\mathrm{\AA}$ , and thus large molecules cannot enter the pores. Several post-synthetic approaches have been developed to overcome this limitation, such as preparing hollow ZIFs and adsorbing molecules onto the external surfaces. However, post-synthesis approaches result in low loadings, and rapid or poorly controlled release of the molecules29,31,32. Hierarchical structures can be introduced into MOFs33-36, which may lead to increased molecular loading. Further, hierarchical mesoporous silica can improve the molecular loading and the efficacy of drug delivery37. However, a certain rate of release of drugs occurs at high pH $\mathrm{\Phi_{\mathscr{P}}H}=8\mathrm{\Phi_{\mathscr{r}}}$ ). It is desirable to construct a pH-responsive drug delivery system that does not release drugs under physiological conditions in the circulation, but that releases the drugs in a controlled manner after uptake of the system into selected cells, such as cancer cells. Such a system would reduce the systemic side effects of chemotherapy, as the active components are released only in the tumor region and not in the general circulation38. However, no simple procedure to construct such a system has been developed.
We report here a simple and rapid process that combines the synthesis of ZIFs and the encapsulation of target organic molecules in one pot. The process has been used for two ZIFs, ZIF-8 and ZIF-67, and with four types of molecule with different functional groups. Electron tomography shows that homogeneously distributed mesopores can be generated within ZIF-8 crystals, and the size of the mesopores can be controlled by the loading of the molecules. We demonstrate that ZIF-8 crystal with encapsulated DOX (denoted DOX@ZIF-8) is a promising pH-responsive drug delivery system for cancer therapy. DOX@ZIF-8 does not release the drug under physiological conditions $\mathrm{(pH\approx7.4)}$ ), and releases it during a period of
7-9 days at $\mathrm{pH}~5.0–6.0$ . This is within the pH range of the endosome and lysosome. The release is achieved by successive decomposition of ZIF-8. The efficacy of the DOX@ZIF-8 system was further demonstrated by in vitro cell assays in three breast cancer cell lines and in primary macrophages. The cellular uptake of DOX $@$ ZIF-8 was demonstrated by confocal microscopy and flow cytometry.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Synthesis of DOX@ZIF-8. Stock solutions of DOX of different concentrations (2, 6 and $10\mathrm{\mg\ml^{-1}})$ were prepared in deionized $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Firstly, $\boldsymbol{0.2\ \mathrm{g}}$ (0.66 mmol) of $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved in $\mathbf{0.8\textrm{g}}$ of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ $\mathrm{(pH~8~}$ , adjusted by NaOH). Then, $4~\mathrm{ml}$ of DOX stock solution was added to the $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ solution. After this had been stirred for $1~\mathrm{min}$ , $\boldsymbol{10}\ \mathrm{g}$ of a solution containing $\ensuremath{2\mathrm{~g~}}$ $(4.62\ \mathrm{mmol})$ of 2-methylimidazole $(2\mathrm{-mim})$ and $\textrm{\tiny8g}$ of deionized $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added dropwise. The reaction mixture was stirred for $15~\mathrm{min}$ . The precipitate was collected by centrifugal separation and washed at least three times with a mixture of ethanol and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The powder product was dried at room temperature under vacuum. The loading amount of DOX was tuned by changing the concentration of the DOX stock solution. DOX-free ZIF-8 was synthesized in a similar way for comparison, using $4~\mathrm{ml}$ of deionized $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .
Synthesis of Dye@ZIF-8, DOX@ZIF-67 and ZIF-8 encapsulated with several components were described in detailed in Supporting Information.
Release of DOX from DOX@ZIF-8 particles at different $p H s.$ A typical release system was prepared by suspending $10~\mathrm{\mg}$ of DOX@ZIF-8 material in $20.0~\mathrm{\ml}$ of buffer solution $\mathrm{\pH}\ 7.4$ , 6.5, 6.0, 5.0 and 4.0, respectively) at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The release system was then maintained at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under shaking (shaking frequency $=150\mathrm{rpm}_{,}$ ). $1\mathrm{ml}$ of release medium was sampled at each time point and UV/Vis spectrophotometry was used to determine the percentage of DOX that had been released, after which the sample was returned to the original release system. In a stepped release experiment, $10~\mathrm{~mg~}$ of $\operatorname{DOX}\ @Z\operatorname{IF}-8$ material was tested in a $20.0~\mathrm{ml}$ , $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ buffer solution of $10\%$ (v/v) FBS at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 7 days. The pH of the solution was then adjusted to 6.5 with dilute HCl $(0.6~\mathrm{M})$ and kept for another 7 days. The $\mathfrak{p H}$ of the solution was again adjusted, stepwise over 3 days, to 6.0, 5.0 and 4.0. The amount of DOX loaded was determined from the UV/Vis absorbance at $479\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The release percentages of DOX were calculated according to the formula, release percentage $(\%)\ =\ m_{r}\ /\ m_{l},$ in which $m_{r}$ is the amount of released DOX while $m_{l}$ is the total amount of loaded DOX.
MTT assays in breast cancer cell lines and primary macrophages. Monocytes were seeded onto 96-well plates with 1 million cells per well, 3 days prior to the test, in order to derive macrophages. MCF-7, MDA-MB-231, and MDA-MB468 cell lines were harvested with trypsin, washed with PBS and re-suspended in Dulbecco’s Modified Eagle Medium (DMEM) at a concentration of $1\times{10}^{5}$ cells per ml. $100~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ per well of the cell suspension was transferred into 96-well plates to pre-culture for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The medium was replaced by a fresh medium that contained the sample (DOX, DOX@ZIF-8, ZIF-8 or ZIF- $8{+}\mathrm{DOX}$ ) at various concentrations. $10~\upmu\upmu\upmu$ of $5~\mathrm{mg~ml^{-1}}$ MTT was then added to each well after designated incubation periods (12 and $24\mathrm{h}$ for the macrophages, and 24 and $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ for the cell lines). After $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ , $100~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of $10\%$ sodium dodecyl sulphate (SDS) solution was introduced into each well. The absorbance at $570~\mathrm{nm}$ was determined with a plate reader after $18\mathrm{h}$ .
Confocal microscopy. MDA-MB-468 cells were seeded at a concentration of $1{\times}\mathrm{\hat{l}}0^{6}$ cells per well onto the surface of cover slips placed in 12-well plates and pre-cultured for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at 37 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The medium was removed and fresh medium that contained free DOX $(0.2~\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{\ml^{-1}})$ or $\mathrm{DOX@ZIF{-}}8$ $1~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ , 20 $w t\%$ loading) was added. After 2, 8 or $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of incubation, the cells were washed twice with PBS and fixed with $4\%$ paraformaldehyde in PBS for 15 minutes at room temperature. The cells were then stained with $2.5~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ of DAPI for 10 minutes and mounted with ProLong $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ Gold anti-fade mounting medium. The stained samples were examined in an FV1000 Olympus confocal microscope at excitation/emission wavelengths of $405/461\ \mathrm{nm}$ for DAPI and $559/572\ \mathrm{nm}$ for DOX. Images were acquired and analyzed with the FluoView FV1000 software.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## One-pot synthesis of target molecule encapsulated in ZIF crystals
The process for one-pot synthesis of MOFs with encapsulated target molecules is presented in Figure 1. Metal ions and target organic molecules (Figure 1a) self-assemble to form coordination polymers (Figure 1b). Organic linkers are added to disassemble the metal ions from the target organic molecules and subsequently form MOFs (Figure 1c) by the assembly of the metal ions and linkers. The target molecules are encapsulated during the formation of MOFs, resulting in hierarchical MOFs. Several molecules with different functional groups (a typical anti-cancer drug DOX, dye molecules rhodamine B, methyl orange and methylene blue) (Figure S1) have been successfully encapsulated into ZIF-8 crystals.
We chose initially to study ZIF-8 as the carrier and DOX, a typical anti-cancer drug used in the treatment of breast and ovarian cancers39,40, as the target molecule. DOX molecules have functional groups that form weak coordination bonds with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions in aqueous media41,42. $\mathrm{DOX}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8$ particles were synthesized in pure aqueous solutions (Figure 2). In a typical synthesis, a solution of $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.66\mathrm{\mmol}$ , $\mathrm{pH}=8$ adjusted by NaOH) and a solution of DOX $(0-30\mathrm{wt\%})$ were mixed and stirred for $1\ \mathrm{\min}$ . A solution of 2- methylimidazole (2-mim, $4.62\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ) was added dropwise. The reaction mixture was stirred for $15\mathrm{min}$ , and $\operatorname{DOX}\ @Z\operatorname{IF}-8$ particles were collected by centrifugal separation (see Table S1, the Experimental section and Supporting Information for detailed descriptions). $\mathrm{DOX@ZIF{-}}8$ particles with loadings of up to $20\%$ were achieved (Figure 2). We confirmed that DOX molecules were coordinated with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions by examining the red shift of the UV/Vis spectrum, and comparing it with that of a free DOX solution, while the solid-state UV/Vis spectrum of DOX $@$ ZIF-8 showed that there was no coordination bond
